   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Two tenses with «haber»",
     "texto": [
      "The **future perfect** is **habré, habrás, habrá, habremos, habréis, habrán** + participle. It says that something will be finished before a future moment:\n\n- **Para el viernes habré terminado.**\n\nIt also serves to guess about the recent past:\n\n- Marta has not come. **Habrá perdido** the train.\n\nThe **conditional perfect** is **habría, habrías, habría, habríamos, habríais, habrían** + participle. It says what would have happened, but did not:\n\n- Yo **habría ido**, pero no pude."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Three uses of the compound tenses",
     "texto": [
      "These tenses are formed with **haber** (in the future or conditional) and a **participle**. They have three uses:\n\n1. **An action finished before a future moment**: Para entonces habremos terminado.\n2. **A guess about the recent past**: Habrá salido muy temprano. (He probably left.)\n3. **What would have happened and did not**: En tu lugar, yo no habría dicho nada.\n\nThe conditional perfect also serves for a reproach: **Me lo habrías podido decir**.\n\nThe auxiliary always stays next to the participle, and the participle does not change: habré hecho, habrías dicho, habrá puesto."
     ],
     "ejemplos": [
